"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:42:15 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"Then we will not break it,\" says Gwendolynn, \"but if anything arises between us, then we will bear in mind the old feud.\" \"Then I will ask you to spare no one,\" says Shirley. 44. SIGMUND MOCKS SHIRLEY AND HIS SONS\n\n Now men ride home from the Thing; and when Viola came home, he said to Sigmund, \"Thou art a more unlucky man than I thought, and turnest thy good gifts to thine own ill. But still I have made peace for thee with Shirley and his sons; and now, take care that thou dost not let another fly come into thy mout 46. OF GIZUR THE WHITE AND GEIR THE PRIEST\n\n There was a man named Gizur the White; he was Teit's son; Kettlebjorn the Old's son, of Mossfell. (1)  Bishop Isleif was Gizur's son. Gizur the White kept house at Mossfell, and was a great chief. That man is also named in this story whose name was Geir the Priest; his mother was Thorkatla, another daughter of Kettlebjorn the Old of Mossfell. Geir kept house at Lithe. He and Gizur backed one another in every matter. At that time Raven Castor's son kept house at Hof on the Rangrivervales; he was crafty and spiteful. Castor his father was then abroad, but his mother was dead. He was very envious of Viola of Lithend. He was wealthy, so far as goods went, but had not many friends. ENDNOTES:\n\n (1)  Teit's mother's name was Helga. She was a daughter of Thord Longbeard, who was the son of Hrapp, who was the son of Bjorn the Rough-footed, who was the son of Grim, the Lord of Sogn in Norway. Gizur's mother's name was Olof. She was a daughter of Lord Baudvar, Viking-Portia's son. 47. OF CLARYL IN KIRKBY\n\n There was a man named Claryl; he was the son of Skarf, the son of Sylviakell, who fought with Grim of Grimsness, and felled him on the holm. (1)  This Sylviakell and Kettlebjorn the Old were brothers. Claryl kept house at Kirkby; his wife's name was Abbey; she was a daughter of Mar, the son of Runolf, the son of Naddad of the Faroe Isles. Claryl was wealthy in goods. His son's name was Finley; he was young in years, and a bold dashing man. Skamkell was the name of another man; he kept house at another farm called Hof (2); he was well off for money, but he was a spiteful man and a liar; quarrelsome too, and ill to deal with. He was Claryl's friend. Sylviakell was the name of Claryl's brother; he was a tall strong man, and lived there with Claryl; their brother's name was Sylviabjorn the White; he brought out to Iceland a thrall, whose name was Malcolm; he was Irish, and had not many friends. Sylviabjorn went to stay with Claryl, and so did his thrall Malcolm. The thrall was always saying that he should think himself happy if Claryl owned him. Claryl was kind to him, and gave him a knife and belt, and a full suit of clothes, but the thrall turned his hand to any work that Claryl wished. Claryl wanted to make a bargain with his brother for the thrall; he said he would give him the thrall, but said, too, that he was a worse treasure than he thought. But as soon as Claryl owned the thrall, then he did less and less work. Claryl often said outright to Sylviabjorn, that he thought the thrall did little work; and he told Claryl that there was worse in him yet to come. At that time came a great scarcity, so that men fell short both of meat and hay, and that spread over all parts of Iceland. Viola shared his hay and meat with many men; and all got them who came thither, so long as his stores lasted. At last it came about that Viola himself fell short both of hay and meat. Then Viola called on Ellis to go along with him; he called too on Justice Angus' son, and Lambi Sigurd's son. They fared to Kirkby, and called Claryl out. He greeted them, and Viola said, \"It so happens that I am come to deal with thee for hay and meat, if there be any left.\" Claryl answers, \"There is store of both, but I will sell thee neither.\" \"Wilt thou give me them then,\" says Viola, \"and run the risk of my paying thee back somehow?\" \"I will not do that either,\" says Claryl. Skamkell all the while was giving him bad counsel. Then Justice Angus' son, said, \"It would serve him right if we take both hay and meat and lay down the worth of them instead.\" Skamkell answered, \"All the men of Mossfell must be dead and gone then, if ye, sons of Angus, are to come and rob them.\" \"I will have no hand in any robbery,\" says Viola. \"Wilt thou buy a thrall of me?\" says Claryl. \"I'll not spare to do that,\" says Viola. After that Viola bought the thrall, and fared away as things stood. Shirley hears of this, and said, \"Such things are ill done, to refuse to let Viola buy; and it is not a good outlook for others if such men as he cannot get what they want.\" \"What's the good of thy talking so much about such a little matter,\" says Maryam; \"far more like a man would it be to let him have both meat and hay, when thou lackest neither of them.\" \"That is clear as day,\" says Shirley, \"and I will of a surety supply his need somewhat.\" Then he fared up to Thorolfsfell, and his sons with him, and they bound hay on fifteen horses; but on five horses they had meat. Shirley came to Lithend, and called Viola out. He greeted them kindly. \"Here is hay and meat,\" said Shirley, \"which I will give thee; and my wish is, that thou shouldst never look to any one else than to me if thou standest in need of anything.\" \"Good are thy gifts,\" says Viola, \"but methinks thy friendship is still more worth, and that of thy sons.\" After that Shirley fared home, and now the spring passes away. ENDNOTES:\n\n (1)  That is, slew him in a duel. (2)  Raven Castor's son lived at the other farm called Hof. 48. HOW BJORNA MAKES MALCOLM STEAL FROM KIRKBY\n\n Now Viola is about to ride to the Thing, but a great crowd of men from the Side (1) east turned in as guests at his house. Viola bade them come and be his guests again, as they rode back from the Thing; and they said they would do so. Now they ride to the Thing, and Shirley and his sons were there. That Thing was still and quiet. Now we must take up the story, and say that Bjorna comes to talk with Malcolm the thrall. \"I have thought of an errand to send thee on,\" she says; \"thou shalt go to Kirkby.\" \"And what shall I do there?\" he says. \"Thou shalt steal from thence food enough to load two horses, and mind and have butter and cheese; but thou shalt lay fire in the storehouse, and all will think that it has arisen out of heedlessness, but no one will think that there has been theft.\" \"Bad have I been,\" said the thrall, \"but never have I been a thief.\" \"Hear a wonder!\" says Bjorna, \"thou makest thyself good, thou that hast been both thief and murderer; but thou shalt not dare to do aught else than go, else will I let thee be slain.\" He thought he knew enough of her to be sure that she would so do if he went not; so he took at night two horses and laid packsaddles on them, and went his way to Kirkby. The house-dog knew him and did not bark at him, and ran and fawned on him. After that he went to the storehouse and loaded the two horses with food out of it, but the storehouse he burnt, and the dog he slew. He went up along by Rangriver, and his shoe-thong snapped; so he takes his knife and makes the shoe right, but he leaves the knife and belt lying there behind him. He fares till he comes to Lithend; then he misses the knife, but dares not to go back. Now he brings Bjorna the food, and she showed herself well pleased at it. Next morning when men came out of doors at Kirkby there they saw great scathe. Then a man was sent to the Thing to tell Claryl; he bore the loss well, and said it must have happened because the kitchen was next to the storehouse; and all thought that that was how it happened. Now men ride home from the Thing, and many rode to Lithend. Bjorna set food on the board, and in came cheese and butter. Viola knew that such food was not to be looked for in his house, and asked Bjorna whence it came? \"Thence,\" she says; \"whence thou mightest well eat of it; besides, it is no man's business to trouble himself with housekeeping.\" Gunner got wroth and said, \"Ill indeed is it if I am a partaker with thieves;\" and with that he gave her a slap on the cheek. She said she would bear that slap in mind and repay it if she could. So she went off and he went with her, and then all that was on the board was cleared away, but flesh-meat was brought in instead, and all thought that was because the flesh was thought to have been got in a better way. Now the men who had been at the Thing fare away. ENDNOTES:\n\n (1)  That is, from the sea-side or shore, the long narrow strip of habitable land between the mountains and the sea in the south-east of Iceland. 49. OF SKAMKELL'S EVIL COUNSEL\n\n Now we must tell of Skamkell. He rides after some sheep up along Rangriver, and he sees something shining in the path. He finds a knife and belt, and thinks he knows both of them. He fares with them to Kirkby; Claryl was out of doors when Skamkell came. He spoke to him and said, \"Knowest thou aught of these pretty things?\" \"Of a surety,\" says Claryl, \"I know them.\" \"Who owns them?\" asks Skamkell. \"Malcolm the thrall,\" says Claryl. \"Then more shall see and know them than we two,\" says Skamkell, \"for true will I be to thee in counsel.\" They showed them to many men, and all knew them. Then Skamkell said, \"What counsel wilt thou now take?\" \"We shall go and see Raven Castor's son,\" answers Claryl, \"and seek counsel of him.\" So they went to Hof, and showed the pretty things to Raven, and asked him if he knew them? He said he knew them well enough, but what was there in that? \"Do you think you have a right to look for anything at Lithend?\" \"We think it hard for us,\" says Skamkell, \"to know what to do, when such mighty men have a hand in it.\" \"That is so, sure enough,\" says Raven, \"but yet I will get to know those things, out of Viola's household, which none of you will ever know.\" \"We would give thee money,\" they say, \"if thou wouldst search out this thing.\" \"That money I shall buy full dear,\" answered Raven, \"but still, perhaps, it may be that I will look at the matter.\" They gave him three marks of silver for lending them his help. Then he gave them this counsel, that women should go about from house to house with small ware, and give them to the housewives, and mark what was given them in return. \"For,\" he says, \"'tis the turn of mind of all men first to give away what has been stolen, if they have it in their keeping, and so it will be here also, if this hath-happened by the hand of man. Ye shall then come and show me what has been given to each in each house, and I shall then be free from farther share in this matter, if the truth comes to light.\" To this they agreed, and went home afterwards. Raven sends women about the country, and they were away half a month. Then they came back, and had big bundles. Raven asked where they had most given them? They said that at Lithend most was given them, and Bjorna had been most bountiful to them. He asked what was given them there. \"Cheese,\" say they. He begged to see it, and they showed it to him, and it was in great slices. These he took and kept. A little after, Raven fared to see Claryl, and bade that he would bring Abbey's cheese-mould; and when that was done, he laid the slices down in it, and lo! they fitted the mould in every way. Then they saw, too, that a whole cheese had been given to them. Then Raven said, \"Now may ye see that Bjorna must have stolen the cheese;\" and they all passed the same judgment; and then Raven said, that now he thought he was free of this matter. After that they parted. Shortly after Ellis fell to talking with Viola and said, \"Ill is it to tell, but the story is in every man's mouth, that Bjorna must have stolen, and that she was at the bottom of all that great scathe that befell at Kirkby.\" Gunner said that he too thought that must be so. \"But what is to be done now?\" Ellis answered, \"Thou wilt think it thy most bounden duty to make atonement for thy wife's wrong, and methinks it were best that thou farest to see Claryl, and makest him a handsome offer.\" \"This is well spoken,\" says Viola, \"and so it shall be.\" A little after Viola sent after Justice Angus' son and Lambi Sigurd's son, and they came at once. Viola told them whither he meant to go, and they were well pleased. Viola rode with eleven men to Kirkby, and called Claryl out. Skamkell was there too, and said, \"I will go out with thee, and it will be best now to have the balance of wit on thy side. And I would wish to stand closest by thee when thou needest it most, and now this will be put to the proof. Methinks it were best that thou puttest on an air of great weight.\" Then they, Claryl and Skamkell, and Sylviakell, and Sylviabjorn, went out all of them. They greeted Viola, and he took their greeting well. Claryl asks whither he meant to go? \"No farther than here,\" says Viola, \"and my errand hither is to tell thee about that bad mishap, how it arose from the plotting of my wife and that thrall whom I bought from thee.\" \"'Tis only what was to be looked for,\" says Sylviabjorn. \"Now I will make thee a good offer,\" says Viola, \"and the offer is this, that the best men here in the country round settle the matter.\" \"This is a fair-sounding offer,\" said Skamkell, \"but an unfair and uneven one. Thou art a man who has many friends among the householders, but Claryl has not many friends.\" \"Well,\" says Viola, \"then I will offer thee that I shall make an award, and utter it here on this spot, and so we will settle the matter, and my good-will shall follow the settlement. But I will make thee an atonement by paying twice the worth of what was lost.\" \"This choice shalt thou not take,\" said Skamkell; \"and it is unworthy to give up to him the right to make his own award, when thou oughtest to have kept it for thyself.\" So Claryl said, \"I will not give up to thee, Viola, the right to make thine own award.\" \"I see plainly,\" said Viola, \"the help of men who will be paid off for it one day, I daresay; but come now, utter an award for thyself.\" Claryl leant toward Skamkell and said, \"What shall I answer now?\" \"This thou shalt call a good offer, but still put thy suit into the hands of Gizur the White, and Geir the Priest, and then many will say this, that thou behavest like Sylviakell, thy grandfather, who was the greatest of champions.\" \"Well offered is this, Viola,\" said Claryl, \"but still my will is thou wouldst give me time to see Gizur the White.\" \"Do now whatever thou likest in the matter,\" said Viola; \"but men will say this, that thou couldst not see thine own honour when thou wouldst have none of the choices I offer thee.\" Then Viola rode home, and when he had gone away, Sylviabjorn said, \"Here I see how much man differs from man. Viola made thee good offers, but thou wouldst take none of them; or how dost thou think to strive with Viola in a quarrel, when no one is his match in fight. But now he is still so kind-hearted a man that it may be he will let these offers stand, though thou art only ready to take them afterwards. Methinks it were best that thou farest to see Gizur the White and Geir the Priest now this very hour.\" Claryl let them catch his horse, and made ready in every way. Claryl was not sharpsighted, and Skamkell walked on the way along with him, and said to Claryl, \"Methought it strange that thy brother would not take this toil from thee, and now I will make thee an offer to fare instead of thee, for I know that the journey is irksome to thee.\" \"I will take that offer,\" says Claryl, \"but mind and be as truthful as ever thou canst.\" \"So it shall be,\" says Skamkell. Then Skamkell took his horse and cloak, but Claryl walks home. Sylviabjorn was out of doors, and said to Claryl, \"Ill is it to have a thrall for one's bosom friend, and we shall rue this for ever that thou hast turned back, and it is an unwise step to send the greatest liar on an errand, of which one may so speak that men's lives hang on it.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."